WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:06.070
77% of prisoners released in America

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.740
will be back behind
bars within five years.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.020
Re-offending is one of
the biggest problems

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.570
facing justice systems around the world.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.200
And nowhere is this more
apparent than in America,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.190
a country that has a quarter
of all the world's prisoners.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.040
Yah.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.390
But can wild horses help?

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.180
You don't feel caged anymore.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.920
Definitely I feel like the
horse is tamin' me sometimes.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.310
These men are prisoners
and horse whisperers.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.290
They teach you a lot about relationships,

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.800
about hard work, perseverance, patience.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.940
At Arizona State Prison,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.162
the inmates trade their

00:00:56.162 --> 00:00:58.370
handcuffs for horses.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.710
They are learning to tame wild mustangs

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.770
as part of a rehabilitation program.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.490
Since it was introduced,
re-offending rates

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.010
amongst those who have
completed the program

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.750
have fallen to 4% over
the last five years.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.500
Mustangs have been a part of

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.870
the American west since it began.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.250
The horses in the wild, those that are on

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.250
Bureau of Land Management
lands, are protected.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.100
But when wild mustang herds grow too big,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.420
they struggle to find enough food

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.350
and can damage the local ecosystem.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.770
To keep the population under control,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.240
around 6,000 are rounded up each year.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.800
Most end up in government holding pens

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.080
at a cost of around $49 million annually.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.920
But some are broken in and sold on

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.390
through horse whispering
programs like this one.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.721
We care for, house,

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.030
and try to get

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.010
horses adopted out.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.470
My name's Anthony.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:09.320
Anthony Garrison.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.700
And my horse name is Flex.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.100
I'm in prison for burglary and drugs.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.430
I've been in since 2010.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.990
I don't get out till February of 2021.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.260
My name's Toby, and the
horse's name is Clyde,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.360
from Bonnie and Clyde.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.930
I'm in for drug possession,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.890
and I've been in for 4 1/2 years.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:36.970
Drug possession.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.150
4 1/2 years for simple possession.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.300
6 1/2 years for simple possession.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.133
Yeah.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.140
Since the 1970s, successive US governments

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.640
have encouraged the harshest
punishment for some offenses,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.110
including mandatory minimum sentences

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.750
for drug-related crimes.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.440
This has seen the prison population

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.760
balloon 500% in 50 years.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.480
This program is a drop in the ocean,

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.520
helping only a small number of inmates,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.140
but it's an imaginative
solution to help solve

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.300
the rehabilitation problem
in American prisons.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:26.110
The incarceration issue in
America, it's obviously huge,

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.830
and the difficulty is
to programs like this

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.060
are really not that easy to develop.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.720
The aim of the Wild Mustang Program

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.820
is to teach prisoners life skills

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.290
that will prevent them from re-offending.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.340
In the long run, this will keep
the prison population down.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.950
If we can reach a handful of inmates,

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.340
and they don't re-offend,
it's a huge saving.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.410
Not just the monetary saving,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.240
but you have a person who has now

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.040
become a productive part of society.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.630
The untamed animals

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.360
show many similar traits to the inmates.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.193
Whoop.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.300
In the wild, these horses are
primarily focused on survival.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.390
Well, a lot of the guys
that come into prison

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.920
have this, kind of, same mindset.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:34.360
There are so many parallels to what

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.490
the horse goes through and
what the inmate goes through.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:40.030
The inmate has to learn
how to live different.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.980
They have to learn how to think different.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.400
The basic principle is you make
the wrong thing harder to do

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.050
and the right thing easier to do.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.430
Of the 50 inmates who have been

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.380
released after completing the program,

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.580
only two have returned to prison.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.700
The inmates are taking responsibility

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.580
for something beyond themselves.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.840
Rehabilitation programs like this

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.260
could help break the
cycle of re-offending.

00:05:15.396 --> 00:05:18.474
♪ Another day, another mile ♪

00:05:18.474 --> 00:05:22.570
♪ You've been gone a good long while ♪

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.180
I'm just not the same person
I was when I first came down.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.020
I don't think the same,
I don't act the same,

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.300
I don't react the same.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.460
It's taught me to help
me with being selfless.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.010
This job isn't about me.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.130
This job is about this horse right here.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.770
As I give up my time, my
patience, and my energy,

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.370
I'm the one who's actually
being taught the lessons.

00:05:45.302 --> 00:05:48.430
♪ Another hill, another climb ♪

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:52.080
♪ Cain't afford to lose no time ♪

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.780
Pioneering programs like this

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.760
are time consuming, hard to establish,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.870
and can only help a
small number of inmates.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:03.810
But they're effective, bucking
the trend of re-offending.

